@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title Layer 4 — hello-world E2E (local model, three targets, plain bridge)

participant "go test -tags=e2e\ne2e/hello_world_test.go" as TEST <<app>>
participant "internal/tmux\ndetached PTY session" as TMUX <<app>>
database "workspace copy\nof e2e/samples" as WS <<db>>
participant "cmd/proveo run <target>\n--egress-mode open --credentials forward\n--local-model <model>" as RUN <<app>>
participant "host Ollama\nhost.docker.internal:11434" as OLLAMA <<host>>
participant "agent container" as CTR <<host>>

TEST ARROW_MAIN WS : copy samples, ""git init""\nDELETE the dangling .env symlink
TEST ARROW_MAIN TMUX : " ""env -u <every provider var> …"" \n(no ""PROVEO_EGRESS_ENV_FILE"" — no credential at all)"
TMUX ARROW_MAIN RUN : PTY — the harness needs ""docker run -it""
RUN ARROW_MAIN CTR : plain bridge + ""ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL"" → Ollama
CTR ARROW_MAIN CTR : entrypoint prints\n" ""PROVEO_MODELS main=… small=…"" "
CTR ARROW_CLOUD OLLAMA : model call (no credential leaves the host)
CTR ARROW_MAIN WS : agent writes ""HELLO_WORLD.txt""\nthrough the bind mount
CTR ARROW_MAIN TMUX : agent replies with the prompt's reply token

TEST ARROW_MAIN TMUX : " ""CaptureAll()"" — pane + scrollback "
TEST ARROW_MAIN WS : poll host paths

note over TEST
  **Three assertions:**
  # //the model answered// — the reply the PROMPT asked for
    appears on the pane. The file alone cannot show the
    inference loop closed, only that //something// wrote it.
  # //bind mount works// — the file exists on the HOST and
    carries this run's unique marker
  # //models bridged// — the ""PROVEO_MODELS"" line names the
    local model in **both** tiers. ""--local-model"" outranks
    every alias, so a tier still holding a cloud default
    means the override only half-applied — and that half
    would quietly bill a provider.

  Per-target paths differ by workspace layout: ""app""
  targets write ""HELLO_WORLD.txt"" at the mount root;
  ""app"" (claudecode) is told to write to
  ""/app/output"" and is checked under ""reports/"".
end note

note over TEST
  **The pane cannot hold the answer, so the run is teed to a
  file.** The agent prints its last words and exits inside a
  single poll interval, and once the session is gone
  ""CaptureAll"" only returns what was captured //before// it
  died — so the reply was always lost even when the model had
  said it. tmux drives ""sh -c '<run> 2>&1 | tee <transcript>'""
  and every assertion reads the transcript: no race, and no
  tmux line wrapping either.

  **Separating the reply from its own echo.** Some entrypoints
  print the prompt on their launch line (""🚀 Launching:
  opencode …$*""), so the reply token is in the transcript
  //before// any inference happened. ""modelSaid"" collapses all
  whitespace, deletes every copy of the instruction, and
  searches what is left: only the model can have produced it.

  **What counts as an answer is deliberately loose.** Either the
  exact reply the prompt asked for, or a mention of the file it
  was told to create. A small local model reproduces a long
  random token byte-exact when //copying it into a tool call//
  — the marker lands in the file perfectly — yet answers the
  human in its own words: gemma4 says "File
  /app/output/HELLO_WORLD\.txt created successfully."
  rather than echoing the token. Demanding the echo would test
  instruction-format compliance, which is the model's business,
  instead of whether the loop closed, which is proveo's.

  **One harness is exempt, by declaration.** ""opencode run""
  prints its launch line and then nothing at all — not the
  reply, not even its own logs despite ""--log-level DEBUG
  --print-logs"". Confirmed from both the pane and the teed
  transcript, so ""repliesOnStdout"" records it in the table
  rather than the lane pretending to assert something it
  cannot read.
end note

note over RUN
  **Why a local model, and why this exact posture.** What this
  suite asserts — mounts, model bridging, a closed inference
  loop — needs //a// model, not a particular vendor's. Billing
  it to a cloud provider made the lane hostage to a credit
  balance, and an empty balance is indistinguishable from a
  broken mount: both read as "no file on the host". **No
  provider credential is supplied at all**, so the run is
  provably credit-free rather than merely cheap.

  ""open"" + ""forward"" is the only posture that reaches the
  HOST's Ollama. Every other tier puts the agent on an
  internal network with DNS blackholed, where the sole
  reachable model server is the sidecar — which on macOS has
  no GPU and generates at CPU speed, roughly 10x slower.

  The workspace ."".env"" is **deleted**, not rewritten. The
  entrypoint sources it //after// docker applies ""-e"", so a
  project ""ARCHITECT_MODEL"" would outrank the ""ollama/*""
  alias ""--local-model"" just bridged in and send the main
  agent to the cloud — the one thing this lane exists to
  avoid.

  Whether a REAL credential still reaches a real provider is a
  different question, asked by
  ""_spec/_paradigms/credential-boundary\.puml"" against an
  endpoint that costs nothing.
end note

note over TMUX
  ""CaptureAll"" reads scrollback: the entrypoint preamble
  scrolls off the visible pane long before the agent
  finishes, so a plain ""capture-pane"" would miss the
  contract line it needs to assert.
end note

note over TEST
  **Third assertion — a clean boot.** A harness can come up
  DEGRADED and still write the file, so the suite scans the
  pane for operator-level failure signatures (MCP handshake
  failures, tracebacks, ""command not found"", entrypoint line
  errors) and fails on them.

  Written after cecli shipped for weeks with its Serena MCP
  server dead on every boot — a package version skew — while
  the side-effect assertion stayed green.
end note

note over CTR
  **Let the run end itself.** The test waits for the tmux
  session to exit before ""t.Cleanup"" kills it: killing the
  pane early SIGHUPs proveo mid-run and strands the egress
  sidecars plus their three networks. Enough leaked subnets
  and Docker refuses new ones outright ("all predefined
  address pools have been fully subnetted"), which then
  looks like a proveo bug in the next unrelated run.
  ""proveo run"" also handles SIGHUP so an abrupt kill still
  tears the topology down.
end note
@enduml
